  "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=2j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/56a5f95d469cb63e20936a6c07971d9e80611d966282cb37d7ddf17fdc761bab.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction.",
